From 4833e2179092c277e657c9d7a0518bed59085527 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 20:51:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Kernels: review of the gradients, inner forces and stiffness
 matrices

An independent review of the computations by kernels found two defects
giving wrong results, both reproduced:

- on the GPU, the Dirichlet boundary conditions wrote the unknowns on the
  host without declaring it to MFEM (`operator[]`): the gradients computed
  by kernels read an outdated copy on the device, and the Newton algorithm
  failed. `updateImposedValues` and `setImposedValuesIncrements` now write
  through `HostReadWrite`;
- the behaviour integrators whose residual or jacobian depend on the
  unknowns (regularisation of Faltus et al.) were accepted by the kernels,
  which only use the thermodynamic forces and the tangent operator blocks:
  the regularisation was silently lost. They are refused.

Also corrected:

- the refusals are made by `setup`: meshes mixing several geometries,
  finite element spaces not supported by the quadrature interpolator of
  MFEM, materials without behaviour integrator;
- the exceptions of the computations of the residual and of the tangent
  operator are reported by `solve` instead of stopping the program;
- accesses on the host declared to MFEM: prediction (crash on the debug
  device), unknowns and essential degrees of freedom before the element
  loops of MFEM (read on the device by the kernels);
- residual by kernels: the element loop of MFEM is skipped when the form
  has no other integrator;
- no partial entry in the cache of the geometric data after an error, and
  checks of the geometric data by the kernels;
- the options of the shared quadrature interpolator of MFEM are restored
  after use.

Tests: refusal of the FBar formulation and of the regularisation of
Faltus et al.; BatchIntegrationTest on the debug device of MFEM and on the
GPU (complete computations with the gradients, the inner forces and the
stiffness matrices computed by kernels, equal to the integration point by
point). 144/144.
